displacement highpassed at 0.075 Hz, and by a boxcar test that rejects DC
steps. The implementation follows A. I. Chung et al., SRL March/April 2019.

## Uses

This is the closest thing available to a direct estimate of a station's
contribution to EPIC's trigger load, calculated from archived data without
needing access to the running system. A station producing large numbers every
hour is feeding EPIC noise that has to be associated and rejected, which costs
processing and can degrade solutions. It is also useful before a station is
brought into ShakeAlert, to estimate what it would contribute. It is
deliberately an approximation: it uses a single channel's archived data and
knows nothing about the state of the real EPIC system, so it should be read as
a station health indicator rather than a prediction of EPIC's actual output.

## Data Analyzed

Traces - one N.S.L.C (Network.Station.Location.Channel) per measurement, from
